WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:07.179
Now that we know how to structure our code to replicate and we can choose which network replication framework we want to use

2
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:12.580
But how do we go about actually testing our game? Do we have to generate a build every time?

3
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.720
Well, the answer to this question is yes, but also no

4
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:22.670
The editor is one of the few special configurations that we can build that will contain both server and client code

5
00:00:23.149 --> 00:00:28.179
The reason for this is that the editor can spin up a server as well as multiple clients to give us a representation

6
00:00:28.179 --> 00:00:29.980
of our networked game

7
00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:36.359
We can also simulate bad ping to see how our gameplay and network systems react when under specific conditions

8
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.060
We can also provide the server with custom launch parameters from within the editor directly

9
00:00:43.219 --> 00:00:49.380
But whilst it will give us a good representation of our network game do not solely rely on this as a method of testing

10
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:56.240
Frequent builds must also be worked into your workflow as they can have discrepancies with what the editor actually presents

11
00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:01.500
So before we end let's take a look at some of the options that we can control from within the editor

12
00:01:01.939 --> 00:01:03.670
There's actually a lot of options in here

13
00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:07.549
But some of the key fields that we need to look at are the number of playing clients

14
00:01:07.769 --> 00:01:13.769
This defines the number of clients to spawn in the game when launched and the editor and listen server count as players whilst the

15
00:01:13.769 --> 00:01:15.739
dedicated server does not

16
00:01:15.739 --> 00:01:23.439
Next we can enable network simulation and if ticked this will enable the options below which allow us to choose different network profiles and

17
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:28.359
Target to simulate them on this is essentially how we go about simulating bad ping for example

18
00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:33.609
Finally, we've got the play net mode and we have one of three options

19
00:01:33.609 --> 00:01:38.390
We can play as a client where we will launch as a client as well as a dedicated server

20
00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:40.909
Which is going to run as a separate process in the background

21
00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:47.180
Next we've got play standalone and it's going to launch us as a standalone game meaning no server at all

22
00:01:48.359 --> 00:01:53.659
Finally, we've got play as listen server and this is going to launch the game with the first client acting as both the server

23
00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:55.659
and its own client

24
00:01:56.019 --> 00:02:01.760
We're then free to just hit play like we would otherwise and the answer is going to spin up as many clients as we requested

25
00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:09.629
And boot up a server in the background to coordinate all of them that concludes introduction to networking in Unreal Engine for programmers

26
00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:14.900
Today we learned about the server client model and how in reels game architecture interacts with it

27
00:02:15.219 --> 00:02:18.389
We looked at replication both functional and variable

28
00:02:18.789 --> 00:02:26.259
We looked at optimization strategies with relevancy and dormancy before moving on to environment queries with the net role and net mode

29
00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.580
We learned about the act replication flow and the replication graph

30
00:02:31.099 --> 00:02:38.620
Before wrapping up with traveling and testing in multiplayer using unreal's inbuilt tools. Thank you so much for watching and have a fantastic day
